
== slab, backend table, R* = 2, 100,000 x 10 = 1,000,000 histories

  answer check: ours 0.860324433, transport.run 0.860324433; flights 7,426,815 vs 7,426,815 -> IDENTICAL

-- Part A: where the wall time goes
  allocate      0.154 s    4.3%
  birth         0.344 s    9.6%
  advance       2.943 s   81.7%
  gather        0.078 s    2.2%
  backend       0.078 s    2.2%
  reduce        0.001 s    0.0%
  (other)       0.002 s    0.1%
  WALL          3.600 s

  what a race calls `seconds` is transport_s + backend_s, which for this run is
  3.443 s against a 3.600 s wall: 4.4% low, all of it the per-batch
  particle allocation. transport.run reported 3.385 s for its own
  execution of the same thing, so run-to-run noise is larger than the gap.

-- Part B: the two kernels inside one flight
  measured on 20,000 real scatter sites from the run above, single-threaded, nanoseconds per call
  _descend, want_near=False             978
  _descend, want_near=True             1085   <- what a ball step needs
    the ball tax (the difference)       107    1.11x
  score_segment                         325
  rand + isotropic                       33

  7,426,815 flights took 2.943 s of _advance, 396 ns each on 4 threads.
  one _descend(False) + score_segment + rng is 1337 ns, of which geometry is 73%.
  that accounts for 84% of the 4-thread budget (assuming perfect scaling, so read it as a lower
  bound on geometry's share).

  every flight pays a full root-down _descend, and every birth pays one too:
  1,000,000 births + 7,426,815 flights. Of those flights 6,275,671 ended in a scatter
  (85%) and 1,151,144 in a surface crossing (15%).
  A scatter does not change the cell, so a cached cell would let 85% of
  the descends start where the particle already is instead of at the root.

  written runs/2026-10-14/loop_breakdown_slab.json

